{"type": "sena", "_data": [{"contents": [{"type": "lesson_name", "content": "Good Deeds"}, {"type": "unit_name", "content": " It Makes a Difference "}, {"type": "position", "content": 9}, {"type": "origin", "content": "stub"}, {"type": "lesson", "content": 13}, {"type": "level", "content": "B2.3"}], "type": "metadata"}, {"contents": [{"type": "test", "content": "false"}], "type": "achievment_test"}, {"contents": [{"type": "primary", "content": " Exchange information about unlikely events or fairly certain conditions that will be fulfilled in present, past or future  in spoken or written texts and in work and social contexts."}, {"type": "secondary_1", "content": "Understands extended speeches, lectures, articles and reports of the professional, academic, or daily life on a wide range of general and work topics, in conversations or presentations, spoken at a natural speed and in circumstances that make it more difficult to hear."}, {"type": "secondary_2", "content": "Understands extended speeches, lectures, articles and reports of the professional, academic, or daily life on a wide range of general and work topics, in conversations or presentations, spoken at a natural speed and in circumstances that make it more difficult to hear."}, {"type": "secondary_3", "content": "Understands extended speeches, lectures, articles and reports of the professional, academic, or daily life on a wide range of general and work topics, in conversations or presentations, spoken at a natural speed and in circumstances that make it more difficult to hear."}, {"type": "secondary_4", "content": "Understands extended speeches, lectures, articles and reports of the professional, academic, or daily life on a wide range of general and work topics, in conversations or presentations, spoken at a natural speed and in circumstances that make it more difficult to hear."}, {"type": "secondary_5", "content": "Understands extended speeches, lectures, articles and reports of the professional, academic, or daily life on a wide range of general and work topics, in conversations or presentations, spoken at a natural speed and in circumstances that make it more difficult to hear."}, {"type": "secondary_6", "content": "Understands extended speeches, lectures, articles and reports of the professional, academic, or daily life on a wide range of general and work topics, in conversations or presentations, spoken at a natural speed and in circumstances that make it more difficult to hear."}, {"type": "secondary_7", "content": "Understands extended speeches, lectures, articles and reports of the professional, academic, or daily life on a wide range of general and work topics, in conversations or presentations, spoken at a natural speed and in circumstances that make it more difficult to hear."}, {"type": "secondary_8", "content": "Understands extended speeches, lectures, articles and reports of the professional, academic, or daily life on a wide range of general and work topics, in conversations or presentations, spoken at a natural speed and in circumstances that make it more difficult to hear."}], "type": "objectives"}, {"type": "background_image", "content": "engenn11le13ob09re1img01.jpg"}, {"contents": [{"type": "heading", "content": "Had Better"}, {"type": "describer", "content": " You use  <span class=\"strong\">had better</span> in spoken English when you want to express that something is a good idea to do or not. You can use it in place of  <span class=\"strong\">should</span> and  <span class=\"strong\">ought to</span>."}, {"type": "text", "content": "In most cases, <span class=\"strong\">had better</span> has a slightly stronger meaning that <span class=\"strong\">should</span> and <span class=\"strong\">ought to</span>. It implies that there might be a negative consequence if you do not do the action."}, {"type": "text", "content": "For example: <br/><span class=\"italic\">You <span class=\"strong\">had better</span> see the dentist about that tooth.</span> (If you don't, it could get much worse) "}, {"type": "text", "content": " You  <span class=\"underline\">do not</span> use <span class=\"strong\">had better</span>  to talk about the past. Instead, you need to use  <span class=\"strong\">should have</span> or <span class=\"strong\">ought to have</span>."}, {"type": "text", "content": "The negative form is  <span class=\"strong\">had better not</span> and in questions,  the subject comes after <span class=\"strong\">had</span>."}, {"type": "text", "content": "For example:  <br/><span class=\"italic\">He'<span class=\"strong\">d better not</span> be late again -  he's already in hot water with the boss.<br/> <span class=\"strong\">Had</span> we <span class=\"strong\">better</span> go earlier?</span>"}, {"type": "example", "content": "Generally, humanitarian tourists <span class=\"strong\">had better</span> be in health and have a desire to explore. "}, {"type": "example", "content": "Since examples of ecotourism abound, a potential eco-tourist <span class=\"strong\">had better</span> learn as much as they can about the trip that awaits them."}, {"type": "example", "content": "You <span class=\"strong\">had better</span> consider a few other factors before signing up with a travel operator. "}, {"type": "example", "content": "Organizations <span class=\"strong\">had better</span> be able to prove they have close and permanent local ties, and their projects respond to needs expressed by the local community."}, {"type": "example", "content": "Individuals interested in this type of experience <span class=\"strong\">had better</span> do research and compare options carefully."}], "type": "learning_points"}, {"contents": [{"type": "heading", "content": " First Conditional and Zero Conditional: Conditional Clause and Main Clause"}, {"type": "describer", "content": "Let's look at the two components of a conditional sentence: the conditional clause (If clause) and the main clause (result clause)."}, {"type": "text", "content": "<span class=\"strong\">Zero Conditional</span><br>  We use <span class=\"strong\">Zero Conditional</span> to talk about things that are always true.<br> The structure of <span class=\"strong\">Zero Conditional</span> is the following:  - If+ Simple Present (if clause)  + Simple Present (result clause). <br>  For example: <br> <span class=\"italic\">If you don't eat, you die. <br> If you heat ice, it melts.</span> <br><br><span class=\"strong\">The First Conditional </span><br> We use the <span class=\"strong\">First Conditional</span> to talk about real possibilities in the present or the future.  <br> The structure of the<span class=\"strong\"> First Conditional</span> is the following: <br>  If + Simple Present, will + Simple Present <br>For example: <br> <span class=\"italic\">If we do not reduce the release of greenhouse gases now, global temperatures will rise.</span><br><br> In the  <span class=\"underline\">conditional clause</span>, you use  <span class=\"italic\">If</span> + Simple Present verb."}, {"type": "text", "content": "For example:<br/><span class=\"italic\"><span class=\"strong\">If</span> you <span class=\"strong\">see</span> the teacher, ...</span>"}, {"type": "text", "content": "In the  <span class=\"underline\">main clauses</span>, sometimes called the result clause, you have three choices.   <ol><li> <span class=\"italic\">will</span> + infinitive (First Conditional) <br/>   <span class=\"italic\"><span class=\"strong\">If</span> you  <span class=\"strong\">see</span> the teacher, you  <span class=\"strong\">will  have to</span> ask her about the test.</span></li>       <li> Present tense (Zero Conditional)<br/>   <span class=\"italic\"><span class=\"strong\">If</span> you  <span class=\"strong\">see</span> the teacher,  the class <span class=\"strong\">is</span> starting.</span></li>      <li>Imperative<br/>   <span class=\"italic\"> <span class=\"strong\">If</span> you  <span class=\"strong\">see</span> the teacher,  <span class=\"strong\">tell</span> him I will be late.</span></li></ol>"}, {"type": "example", "content": "If you are thinking about going on a trip, why not do more than just visit temples or suntan on the beach?"}, {"type": "example", "content": "If you are interested in environmental protection, ecotourism is another way of showing that you are a principled tourist. "}, {"type": "example", "content": "Additionally, if you choose a tour package that contains an element of volunteering, you may be buying a lower quality tour at a higher price."}, {"type": "example", "content": "If you travel responsibly, you can make a difference for both yourself and the local community.  "}], "type": "learning_points"}, {"contents": [{"type": "name", "content": "ecotourism"}, {"type": "definition", "content": "a form of travel that includes learning about and protection of the natural world"}, {"type": "part_speech", "content": "(noun)"}, {"type": "example", "content": "Example: If you are interested in environmental protection, ecotourism is another way of showing that you are a principled tourist. "}, {"type": "audio", "content": "engenn11le13ob09aud01"}], "type": "target_word"}, {"contents": [{"type": "text", "content": " These are words you use when you talk about ecotourism."}], "type": "target_word_describer"}, {"contents": [{"type": "name", "content": "landmark"}, {"type": "definition", "content": " a natural or human-made structure that is distinct from everything around"}, {"type": "part_speech", "content": "(noun)"}, {"type": "example", "content": "Example: Student tourists learn about environmental protection and build awareness of cultural landmarks."}, {"type": "audio", "content": "engenn11le13ob09aud02"}], "type": "target_word"}, {"contents": [{"type": "name", "content": "gap year "}, {"type": "definition", "content": "one year many students take to work or travel after high school before entering university"}, {"type": "part_speech", "content": "(noun)"}, {"type": "example", "content": "Example: Some programs involve gap year travel for secondary school leavers."}, {"type": "audio", "content": "engenn11le13ob09aud04"}], "type": "target_word"}, {"contents": [{"type": "name", "content": "criteria"}, {"type": "definition", "content": "a detailed list of actions or descriptions of a product that are used for evaluation purposes"}, {"type": "part_speech", "content": "(noun)"}, {"type": "example", "content": "Example: TIES has created a set of criteria that can be used to determine if a travel project can be considered ecotourism. "}, {"type": "audio", "content": "engenn11le13ob09aud03"}], "type": "target_word"}, {"contents": [{"type": "name", "content": "tour package"}, {"type": "definition", "content": "travel arrangements, often including transportation, food, accommodation and excursions, grouped and sold by a travel agency"}, {"type": "part_speech", "content": "(noun)"}, {"type": "example", "content": "Example: Additionally, if you choose a tour package that contains an element of volunteering, you may be buying a lower quality tour at a higher price."}, {"type": "audio", "content": "engenn11le13ob09aud05"}], "type": "target_word"}, {"contents": [{"type": "text", "content": "Choose a destination for a humanitarian trip or an ecotourism experience. Go online for ideas."}, {"type": "text", "content": "Read information about your choice of destination. Find 2 - 3 different sources of information."}, {"type": "text", "content": "Prepare a brochure a travel agency could use to sell the trip. In the brochure, write 3 - 4 paragraphs describing the trip. Answer the questions: what will the person do and see? How will the trip be useful to the local population?"}, {"type": "text", "content": "Include photos and images in your brochure."}, {"type": "text", "content": "Invent details for the trip, like price and details related to accommodation, food and transportation."}, {"type": "text", "content": "Present your brochure to a partner. In your presentation, invit